    meditation:
      "He has booked the four o'clock and he has brought the contract with him. The cover email said \"quick read\" and the subject line ended in an exclamation point. He has already signed two of the three signature blocks; he is waiting for you to clear the third. He calls it due diligence. What he wants is your initials.\n\nThis is the meeting that Solomon was thinking of when he wrote that the wounds of a friend are better than the kisses of an enemy. Most professional unhappiness begins here, in the small gap between what the client has booked you for and what the client actually needs. He came for permission. Counsel is the other thing. Counsel is what your bar card obliges you to give him, and what he will, three weeks from now, either thank you for or never forgive you for, depending on whether you survive the next hour.\n\nThe Latin is consilium and the discipline is older than your firm and older than the common law. Aquinas places counsel among the acts of charity, which startles the modern lawyer. We are used to thinking of advice as a service we sell by the hour. Aquinas treats it as a wound we inflict on a friend for his good. \"Fraternal correction properly so called is an act of charity, even as the curing of a sick brother's body belongs to charity.\" Note the verb: curing. The lawyer who tells the truth at four o'clock is a surgeon, not a salesman. Pretending otherwise is the first failure of nerve.\n\nThe second failure is subtler. It is the practiced art of saying no in a voice that sounds like yes. \"There are some considerations we should think through.\" \"This is aggressive but defensible.\" \"I want to flag a few items.\" The client, who is not stupid, hears the flag and notes that you did not actually lower the boom, and signs anyway, and tells his board that counsel reviewed it. You have not lied. You have done something a little worse than lying: you have manufactured a record that you can hide behind when the matter goes sideways, while leaving the client in exactly the position he came in. The kiss of the enemy in Proverbs is not malicious. It is merely deceitful. It is the kiss that pretends to be friendship and is not.\n\nWhat does the hard word actually sound like. It sounds like this: \"I cannot sign off on section 7. If you do it anyway you will not have my advice behind you. I will tell you why I think it will fail, and then if you want to proceed, you will need other counsel.\" That sentence is one of the most expensive sentences in the English language. It costs the client's affection in the short run, the engagement letter sometimes, and the easy weekend always. It also costs nothing compared to what it saves: him, mostly; yourself, partly; the bar, in some small way that no one will ever measure.\n\nThis week sits with consilium because the lawyer's life is built on it and the lawyer's life is constantly tempted to abandon it. The week's authors are not chosen at random. Aquinas treats counsel as both a virtue and a gift of the Holy Spirit, which is to say, sometimes you will see clearly without effort and sometimes you will agonize for a week, and both are part of the same craft. Thomas More, who lost his head over a counsel he refused to give, wrote letters about it to his daughter and to Cromwell that read like the inside of a careful man's prayer. We will sit with those. We will sit with Cicero on what a friend's counsel is for, and with Augustine on the institutional sin of consensus advice, and with the truth that the hardest part of this work is not knowing what to say but bringing yourself to say it.\n\nNotice today how often you reach for the soft formulation. Notice the phrases. \"Optically.\" \"Defensible.\" \"Within the four corners.\" Each of these is sometimes the right word and each of these is often a substitute for the right word, which is no. The advocate who can no longer hear the difference has lost the use of his tongue for what tongues were given for.\n\nNotice too that you are not asked to be cruel. Proverbs says open rebuke; it does not say loud rebuke. The surgeon does not shout at the patient. He says, this is what we have found, and this is what it means, and this is what we should do. He does not enjoy the cut. He makes it because the alternative is worse. The client at four o'clock has paid you for that cut. He does not know yet that he has paid you for it. He thinks he has paid you for the green light. He is wrong. You have an entire week to relearn the difference, and to find again, under the layers of professional softening, the voice that says simply: no, and here is why, and I am still your lawyer, and I would rather you be angry with me at six than ruined at sixty.\n\nA last thing. The client who came for permission is, very often, the client you most want to please. He pays on time. He refers his friends. He brings his wife to the firm Christmas party. None of this matters at four o'clock. The wounds of a friend are not less wounding because the friend is grateful for them; they are wounds because they hurt. Give them honestly. They are what you were trained for.",
