    meditation:
      "There is a particular phone call that comes around nine at night. A mother whose son has been arrested that afternoon. The charges are serious. She has not slept. She has the number of someone who knows you, and she is calling to ask, in the same breath, three impossible questions: what should we do, can you help, and will he be all right. You are not the criminal lawyer she needs. You can name two who are. But she is not, in this moment, ready for names. She is ready for a voice that does not rush her off the line.\n\nSirach is a book the Reformation cut out of most Bibles, which is a pity, because no other writer in the canon teaches the lawyer better. \"There is one that holdeth his peace, knowing the proper time.\" The verse distinguishes two silences. There is the silence of the man who has nothing to say, which is the silence of an empty room. And there is the silence of the man who has everything to say and is waiting, because the moment for saying it has not arrived. The second silence is a virtue. It is in fact part of counsel, and you cannot give counsel without it.\n\nThe young associate's instinct is the opposite. The associate is trained for issue-spotting and is rewarded for answers, and so the associate, hearing the mother on the phone, begins to triage. He will list bail hearings and arraignments and the prosecutor's likely first move. He may even, in his nervousness, begin to describe sentencing ranges. He thinks he is helping. He is in fact intruding into a wound that has not yet finished bleeding. She did not call for a sentencing range. She called because the world has come apart, and because someone she trusts gave her your number, and what she needs from you for the first three minutes is the simple fact that you are still on the line.\n\nAmbrose, writing for the clergy of fourth-century Milan, told them that the first duty was to know the value of silence and the next to know when to speak and when to be silent. He was a former Roman governor and he knew exactly what kind of magistrate or advocate his readers would become if they did not learn this. The lawyer who has not learned to be silent will counsel only what he is ready to say at the moment he is asked, which is to say, he will counsel mostly his own anxiety. The lawyer who has learned will counsel from a place the client has not yet arrived at, and will wait there.\n\nThe M&A pre-close call is the corporate version of the same phone. The CFO is on speaker; the bankers are on; the seller's counsel is on; everyone has flown in. There is a problem in the disclosure schedule that everyone wishes you would describe as small. They are looking at you on the screen. They want a sentence that lets the call end. The longer you wait, the louder the silence becomes. This is the moment to wait. Not theatrically. Not to be cruel. To wait because the right sentence is forming and the wrong sentence is, right now, easier and faster. The bankers will not thank you for the pause. The client will, in two weeks, in private, if you say the right thing at the end of it.\n\nThere is a third silence, the worst one, which Sirach also names: the silence of the man who knoweth not what to say. The lawyer who has not done the work, who has not read the document, who is bluffing. This silence is contemptible and the room can always tell. The remedy is not to fill it with talk. The remedy is to say, I do not know yet; I will tell you tomorrow; do nothing until I do. This is its own kind of counsel and is often the correct one. The young lawyer is afraid that to say \"I don't know\" is to lose authority. The truth is the opposite. The lawyer who has said \"I don't know\" once, and then come back with the answer, is the lawyer the client trusts at four o'clock when the contract arrives.\n\nThe parish council is the same phone again, in a different key. They want you to bless their preferred answer about the rental agreement, or the bylaws, or the disgruntled employee. They are seven volunteers in a basement and they have agreed already, and the only thing remaining is to enlist you. Here, too, the temptation is to fill the silence. To smooth it. To say something genial that sounds like agreement. Ambrose's instruction holds: know the value of silence first. Let them finish describing their decision. Let the pause sit. Then say what you actually think, in three sentences, with the same care you would use for any paying client, and bear the awkwardness of being the one who said no in a room that had already voted yes.\n\nA practical rule, then, for the week. When the question is hard and the room is hot, count to four before you speak. Not because four seconds is magic but because four seconds is long enough for the cheap answer to evaporate. If the right sentence has not formed by the count of four, say so, and ask the better question, or ask for a day. The client does not need you to be fast. The client needs you to be right. The lawyer who has confused the two is the lawyer whose practice is full and whose counsel is empty.\n\nSilence is a kind of speech. The mother on the phone at nine at night does not need a sentencing range. She needs you to ask, gently, whether she has eaten. She needs you to tell her you will call back at seven in the morning with the names of two people who do this work. She needs to hear, in your slow breath at the other end of the line, that the world she fell into this afternoon is, in fact, survivable. Then, in the morning, give the names. The silence is the counsel. The names are its fruit.",
