    excerpt: {
      workId: "more-utopia",
      author: "Thomas More",
      citation: "Letter to Margaret Roper from the Tower, 1534",
      text: "I will not mistrust him, Meg, though I shall feel myself weakening and on the verge of being overcome with fear. I shall remember how Saint Peter at a blast of wind began to sink because of his lack of faith, and I shall do as he did: call upon Christ and pray to him for help. And then I trust he shall place his holy hand on me and in the stormy seas hold me up from drowning.",
    },
    meditation:
      "More wrote three kinds of letters in his last year and we should read all three, because between them they show what counsel looks like when the cost of it is real. He wrote to his daughter Margaret, who was begging him to take the oath. He wrote to Cromwell, who was looking for an opening. And he wrote, earlier and at greater length, to Henry himself, in the years when he believed the king could still be reasoned with. The arc from the first to the last is the arc of a lawyer learning that some clients will not be saved, and that the counsel must be given anyway.\n\nThe letters to Henry are the easiest to romanticize and the hardest to read straight. More had been a royal councillor for years before he became Chancellor. The work of those letters is not heroic. It is careful, patient, technically dense, and full of the polite Latin formulae that a man trained at New Inn used to soften the hard word. He flatters the king at the top and the bottom of the page and then, in the middle, tells him what he will not do. The lawyer who has never written that kind of memo does not know what it is to be the only one in the room who will say so on paper. The lawyer who has written it once never forgets the night before he sent it.\n\nThe letter to Cromwell, in 1534, is colder and more useful. Cromwell wanted a small concession; he was building a case and he needed only the door to be open. More refused with a precision that any practicing lawyer should study. He did not denounce the oath. He did not lecture Cromwell. He said, in essence, I will not say what I do not believe, and I will not give a reason that can be turned against another. He understood that the man across the table was assembling exhibits. He gave him nothing. He also did not pretend friendship he no longer felt, nor enmity he had not earned. This is one of the great professional letters in the language and the reason for that is that More was a lawyer first and a saint by consequence. The sainthood is downstream of the craft.\n\nThe letters to Margaret are the hardest, because she was right and he knew she was right and he refused her anyway. She was telling him what every loving family tells the lawyer who is about to ruin them: take the oath, come home, the cause is not worth it, we need you. He answered her not by minimizing the cost but by naming it. \"I will not mistrust him, Meg, though I shall feel myself weakening and on the verge of being overcome with fear.\" The sentence does not pretend that he is not afraid. It says only what he will do with the fear. Counsel given to a daughter is not different in kind from counsel given to a client; it is harder because the client cannot also be your child. More managed both at once, which is why we still read him.\n\nProverbs 24 sits behind all three letters. \"Deliver them that are led to death: and those that are drawn to death forbear not to deliver.\" The verse is read narrowly as a charge to capital defense lawyers; it is in fact a charge to anyone whose counsel could save another from a course that will destroy him. The startling clause is the second one. \"If thou say: I have not strength enough: he that seeth into the heart, he understandeth.\" The Holy Spirit, through the writer of Proverbs, knows that the lawyer's plea of weakness is exactly the plea the lawyer will offer. He looks past it. The verse does not condemn the lawyer who is afraid. It refuses the lawyer who hides behind his fear.\n\nThis is the use of More for the rest of us. Most of our clients are not Henry. Most of our refusals do not end in a scaffold. The structure is the same. The founder who wants you to bless a tax position that will not survive a competent audit. The general counsel who wants you to opine that the disclosure is sufficient when you both know it is not. The cousin at the family dinner who wants you to say the patriarch's will is fine. In each case there is a Cromwell at your elbow and a Margaret at home and a Henry across the desk, and the question is whether you will write the letter that costs you the relationship rather than the letter that costs the client his soul.\n\nNotice that More did not write any of those letters alone. He had his confessor. He had his books. He had a daughter who, even while she begged him to relent, was the first person he read his answers to. The Christian lawyer who tries to do this work without a confessor, without books, and without one or two human beings who know what he is wrestling with, will sooner or later find that the soft answer has crept in unnoticed and become his ordinary voice. More is a saint not because he was strong but because he had the right friends and used them. Get the friends. Use them.\n\nA practical exercise. Take a memo or an email you sent last quarter where you knew the right answer was harder than the answer you wrote. Read it as if Cromwell were reading it. Where did you leave the door open. Where did you flatter. Where, in the polite Latin, did you let the client off the hook. Now draft, for yourself, the version you would have sent if you had had More's nerve. Do not send it. Keep it. The point of the exercise is to learn the difference between the two voices, so that next quarter, when the next memo comes due, you can write the better one the first time.\n\nMore was a lawyer all the way down. His holiness was not in spite of the craft. It was the craft, perfected to the point where it cost him everything. We do not need to be martyrs. We need to learn from one.",
